== 7. The console noise, and why no earlier probe could see it ==
Two designed "nothing here" answers are 4xx, so Chrome files a red console line for
each. Both are functionally correct and neither is suppressible from JS.

(a) GET /api/auth/me -> 401 for an anonymous visitor.
    app/api/auth/me/route.ts: `if (!token) return authError("UNAUTHORIZED", …, 401)`
    Fetched after hydration by hooks/usePublicSession.ts:51, whose own comment says
    "401 is the normal signed-out case, not an error worth reporting" — the header
    ships signed-out markup and upgrades after hydration precisely so a server
    cookie read does not opt ~30 static routes out of prerendering.
    Measured: 36 occurrences = 4 pages x 9 widths (/, /tools, /pricing, /blog).
    /login, /signup and /editor do not render that header, hence errs=0.

(b) GET /api/workspaces/…/documents/…/editor-state -> 404 EDITOR_STATE_UNAVAILABLE
    for a version with no saved scene (route.ts:110-126, "Deliberately
    undifferentiated"). Measured once, by premium gate G5:
      PASS  G5 no console errors  — clean (1 network entries ignored:
            https://192.168.0.175:3001/api/workspaces/<id>/documents/<id>/editor-state?organizationId=<id>
            Failed to load resource: the …)

Why every earlier measurement missed it: premium-ui-ux-probe.mjs's main() calls
signUp(ctx) before the scenario loop under --auth, so /api/auth/me answers 200 in
scenarios A/B/I — 10 of its 11 console gates ignored exactly 0 network entries.
responsive-qa.mjs is always anonymous and had never been included in an evidence
package before this stage. And probe-browser.mjs splits jsErrors from netErrors
(Chrome files "Failed to load resource: 401" under Log.entryAdded source=network),
which is why Stage 11's "0 JS errors" and these 36 lines are both true statements
about the same build.

Filed as P3, not fixed here: changing an auth endpoint's status code is not a
clearly low-risk edit during acceptance. Owner row.

== 8. 19-app-error is NOT EXERCISED, and the probe's hint for it is stale ==
The probe suggests reaching the error boundary by starting with a broken
DATABASE_URL. That technique is obsolete. Measured on a throwaway origin
(PORT=3004, started by hand rather than through scripts/restart-origin.sh so the
live origin's .next/static was never touched):

  $ DATABASE_URL=file:/tmp/pa-stage12-broken/nonexistent-dir/x.db … node ingress/server.mjs
  $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} ' http://127.0.0.1:3004/ /workspaces \
      /api/health /api/health/ready
  503 503 503 503     body: {"error":"Server is not accepting requests."}

src/infrastructure/config/instanceLease.ts:95-170 — tryAcquire() throws on a
genuine database failure and beat()'s catch keeps status "pending" — while
ingress/guard.mjs:107-129 serves only `SERVING = new Set(["held","disabled"])`.
So an unusable database now returns 503 on EVERY path and the Next application is
never invoked: no error boundary renders, and there is nothing to screenshot.
Two consequences recorded for Stage 13, not decided here:
  - The guard's own comment says an operator reads the difference in
    /api/health/ready's `instance` field. In exactly the states that matter that
    endpoint is one of the 503s, so the field is unreachable.
  - Dockerfile:105-106 healthchecks /api/health, which a lease-unheld process
    refuses. The container is therefore marked unhealthy for a lease it may be
    correctly waiting on. docker-compose.yml defines no healthcheck of its own.
Live listeners re-verified 200 afterwards; /tmp/pa-stage12-broken removed.

